picolibc 1.8.12 aligns malloc's result to _Alignof(max_align_t), which
on m68k is TWO BYTES -- the m68k ABI aligns even a double to 2. That is
conforming, and it breaks a great deal of real software: anything that
stores flags in the low bits of a pointer it got from malloc assumes
what glibc, musl and the BSDs all guarantee, which is 2 * sizeof(size_t).

CPython is one. Its garbage collector tags the low two bits of the list
pointers in every object's GC header, and its first collection followed
a corrupted pointer into nowhere -- a bus error at a wild address, on
`python3 -c "print(1+1)"`, on a build that was otherwise complete.

This makes the alignment the larger of the two. Not specific to m68k,
but nothing else picolibc targets has an alignment small enough to
notice.

diff -ru a/libc/stdlib/local-malloc.h b/libc/stdlib/local-malloc.h
--- a/libc/stdlib/local-malloc.h	2026-09-22 16:16:01.278319421 -0600
+++ b/libc/stdlib/local-malloc.h	2026-09-22 16:16:01.291455176 -0600
@@ -109,9 +109,28 @@
     struct malloc_chunk *next;
 } chunk_t;
 
-/* Alignment of allocated chunk. Compute the alignment required from a
- * range of types */
-#define MALLOC_CHUNK_ALIGN _Alignof(align_chunk_t)
+/*
+ * Alignment of an allocated chunk: the strictest of any ordinary type,
+ * but NEVER LESS THAN 2 * sizeof(size_t), which is what glibc, musl and
+ * the BSDs all guarantee and what the world's software assumes.
+ *
+ * The C standard only asks for an alignment suitable for any type with
+ * a fundamental alignment, and on m68k that is TWO BYTES -- the ABI
+ * aligns even a double to 2. A conforming malloc may therefore return
+ * an odd-word address there, and picolibc's did. Software that stores
+ * flags in the low bits of a pointer it got from malloc then corrupts
+ * itself: CPython's garbage collector tags the low two bits of every
+ * object's list pointers, and died on its first collection with a
+ * 2-byte-aligned object in the list.
+ *
+ * The cost is a few bytes per allocation on targets whose natural
+ * alignment is smaller. The alternative is being right in law and
+ * broken in fact.
+ */
+#define MALLOC_MIN_ALIGN   (2 * sizeof(size_t))
+#define MALLOC_CHUNK_ALIGN                                          \
+    (_Alignof(align_chunk_t) > MALLOC_MIN_ALIGN                     \
+     ? _Alignof(align_chunk_t) : MALLOC_MIN_ALIGN)
 
 /* Alignment of the header. Never larger than MALLOC_CHUNK_ALIGN, but
  * may be smaller on some targets when size_t is smaller than
